Betsy Schwartz writes:

> In addition, I look for the application of Extropian ideas to the *near*
> future. The frequent list discussions of PPL, et al, strike me as a form of
> social science fiction, akin to the discussions of the Post-Singularity
> world. We don't really know what true Anarchy would be like, and we don't
> know what discoveries we will make that might change our perception of our
> long-term goals.

I contend that "true anarchy" is all around us. It's in how we deal
with our friends, where there is no "ruler" who can coerce us into
noncensual behaviors. It's in how we choose books to read, food to
eat, movies to see, places to walk, and a multitude of other things in
which a ruler or a government has fairly minimal effect. Thus, anarcho
capitalists are not asking for some idyllic state that has never been
realized....they just want governments out of our economic lives, out
of sending us to die in Somalia and Bosnia, out of boosting some
indutries while penalizing others, and out of generally interfering in
what folks would do without government to threaten others.

>    I know that many people here are doing serious and productive work on
> inventions, both physical and memetic. As new technological inventions are
> realized, it is easy to see how they are introduced into the marketplace.
> It is harder for me to see how new ideas are introduced. 

Well, you've apparently missed several of the interesting threads on
start-ups, on the Newton, on new gadgets, and even on mundane things
like shoes. In fact, I disagree with the notion that the List spends
its time discussing distant abstractions....I think in fact we spend a
disproportionate amount of time on everyday matters like vitamins,
children, guns, diet, exercise, reading, hot tubs, and the like. Take
a look at a list of the last, say, 100 messages to the List.

>     For example, my gripe with many Libertarian politicians is that,
> whether you agree or disagree with their view of an ideal government, they 
> often seem to be aiming for a partial breakdown of the existing system
> without causing enough change for their ideal market to come into play.
> Breaking random bits of the current system can cause more damage than the
> original system did. For example, our governor has successfully closed many
> institutions for the handicapped, retarded, and mentally ill. People here

Sounds good to me. The retarded and handicapped are the problems of
their family, not my problem. I worked hard for my money and I don't
want to see it stolen from me to be given to drooling idiots and drug
addicts. Most of the "welfare" cases have self-selected their
behavior. They were, for example, goofing off in high school when I
was slaving away at science fair projects, or getting high on sixpacks
of beer while I was reading and expanding my opportunities.

Now they refuse to take jobs at fast food places (didn't Betsy and I
argue this exact point exactly a year ago, with Betsy giving us a sob
story about the depressed and jobless friend of hers?).

I say, let them die. While I will not countenance the creation of
concentration camps to dispose of this human garbage, I certainly see
that these ghetto criminal, uneducabel losers, and shiftless drifters
have no place in any society I envision. With the Extropian
technologies widening the gap between those with intitiative and
intelligence, and those without one or the other, I see this piles of
humnan detritus being left permanently behind.

This is a _much_ more radical view that simple and often wimpy
Libertarianism, so I suspect nurturing and caring folks who think
Libertarianism is too harsh are indeed on the wrong discussion list.

(Which I recall was Betsy's conclusion a year ago, when she decided to
create her own List, Future-Tech or something like that.)

Personally, I welcome Betsy's presence on this list--not to imply that
my welcome should mean anything more than it does--but there is a huge
gulf in views.

> have explained how, if we had the ideal anarchist or Libertarian system, we
> would be able to help these people (or protect ourselves from them,
> depending on who's writing.) But, we don't have that right now, we just
> have the old system with one piece knocked out, accelerating both the
> suffering of the people who lost their support, and the cost in both danger
> and dollars to everyone else.

No, this is "debating the basics." Please read Charles Murray's
"Losing Ground," a seminal book which is even gaining some currency
amongst welfare reformers in the Clinton Administration. Murray's
point, compelling documented, is that the welfare/AFDC system of the
last 30 years has created the current Titanic-scale disaster of human
suffering as well as the nearly complete marginalization of the entire
American black race.

Knocking out the old system in a "Big Bang" sudden transition might
cause the deaths by exposure and starvation of some number of folks.
Too bad, abstractly, but it'll be better to go "cold turkey" and then
begin to put people back in gainful employment.

For those who can't or won't cope, good riddance. The free ride is
over.

Time to rid ourselves of the welfare thieves who steal our money and
breed like maggots in their ghetto warrens.

>   So, what I'd like to see more of is: how do we get from here to there? 
