Finally had a chance to carefully review your posting to
extropians re: Internet via catv.  Cable system head ends
may very well serve hundreds of thousands of subscribers.
However, modern systems are a 2 tier distribution, with
'fiber to the curb' from the head end to a neighborhood,
and then coaxial cable from the 'node' to the individual
subscriber terminations.  The coax is a shared access
medium, but the fiber is not: it is point to point, with
one end being the CATV system head end and the other
being the neighborhood 'node'.  This permits CATV MANs
to be segmented, if the architecture and technology
support it.  Feel free to forward this to the list
if you think anyone is likely to care, we seem to be
off this topic now.
