Subject: Misunderstanding Statistics (was VOUDON: The Bates Method)

Andy Wilson writes:

>Here's a scenario for you:  what if only one person ever showed improved
>vision through exercises,  and was in a large sample population of a 
>study. Would the fact that their improvement was statistically 
>insignificant make their eyes all of a sudden get worse?

HUH?!

Lack of  statistical significance means that the improvement was likely due 
to some other factor.  Even if the improvement were due to the treatment 
factor (e.g., Bates exercises), the effect is not general enough to suggest 
that others will obtain the same results from that treatment.

-- Simon!


