An interesting, if brief, comment. 

Not knowing precisely what Nancy has in mind here, I'll speculate.

Yes, I think a case could be made along the lines of what Charles
Murray made in "Losing Ground," that most of the urban poor have
essentially been victimized by well-meaning but systemically flawed
government handout programs. Building acres of "Projects" in the
northern inner cities of Detroit, Chicago, Boston, New York, etc., was
ultimately a disaster, with the Projects serving as de factor
concentration camps for blacks and Hispanics and cutting them off from
the normal sociey and from suburban job opportuniies.

In a tragic irony, the great wave of black immigration into the
northern industrial cities took place during and after the second
world war, reaching a peak in the late 1950s and early 1960s. The
irony was that they were arriving just as the new wave of corporate
growth was developing in the suburbs, in the areas outside the inner
city core. The aerospace, computer, defense, and electronics
businesses, plus all the construction jobs in building these suburbs,
in building the interstate highway system, and in staffing the
millions of service and teaching jobs that appeared in the suburbs.

So, we promised rural blacks the largesse of the State in the innner
cities, lured them into ghettoes and projects in the icy north (most
were from the South, of course), and put them into a social system in
which marriage was a lose (loss of benefits, housing), in which few
jobs existed, and in which the effect was that of a concentration
camp.

I still think individuals must choose their won paths, and cannot
place the blame on others unless they were physically coerced, but a
case could certainly be made that the urban poor of today can thank
the government for their troubles.

However, the cure is most definitely more government programs, be they
job training programs or more handouts. The cure is to tell people the
free ride will end in, say, 6 months. After that, no welfare, no AFDC,
no food stamps, no subsidized tenements, no nothing.

Some fraction of them will flee the barren inner cities, some fraction
will return perhaps to Africa or other continents, some fraction will
turn to even more violent crime and will have to be dealt with by
vigilantes, and some fraction will simply starve to death.

A monument to government mendacity and foolishness.


